CAT = "cawhmisBasics"; SEG = "BAS"
Q = [
('What does WHMIS stand for?',
 'Workplace Hazardous Materials Information System', ['Workplace Health and Material Inspection Standard', 'Worker Hazard Management Information Service', 'Workplace Hazardous Mixtures Identification Scheme'],
 "WHMIS is the Workplace Hazardous Materials Information System, Canada's national hazard communication standard for hazardous products used at work."),
('What international system is current WHMIS aligned with?',
 'The GHS', ['The TDG Regulations', 'ISO 45001', 'The OSHA HCS only'],
 'WHMIS 2015 adopted the Globally Harmonized System of Classification and Labelling of Chemicals (GHS). Transportation of Dangerous Goods is a separate system for products in transport.'),
('Which federal department is responsible for the supplier rules in WHMIS?',
 'Health Canada', ['Transport Canada', 'Environment Canada', 'Employment and Social Development Canada'],
 'Health Canada administers the Hazardous Products Act and Hazardous Products Regulations, which set the classification, label and SDS duties for suppliers.'),
('Which federal law and regulation set the WHMIS rules for suppliers?',
 'Hazardous Products Act and Regulations', ['Canada Labour Code and its regulations', 'TDG Act and Regulations', 'Canada Consumer Product Safety Act'],
 "Suppliers' duties come from the Hazardous Products Act (HPA) and the Hazardous Products Regulations (HPR). Workplace duties come from occupational health and safety laws."),
('Who enforces WHMIS duties in a provincially regulated workplace?',
 'The provincial OHS regulator', ['Health Canada inspectors only', 'The local fire department', "The product's supplier"],
 'Employer and worker WHMIS duties are set by provincial, territorial or federal OHS law, and the matching OHS regulator enforces them in the workplace.'),
('What are the main elements of WHMIS?',
 'Labels, SDSs and training', ['Labels, permits and fire audits', 'SDSs, audits and medical exams', 'Placards, permits and air tests'],
 'WHMIS rests on hazard classification, labels, safety data sheets and worker education and training. Permits and medical exams are not WHMIS elements.'),
('Who must classify a hazardous product and provide its label and SDS?',
 'The supplier', ['The worker using it', 'The joint health and safety committee', 'The provincial OHS regulator'],
 'Suppliers (manufacturers, importers and distributors) classify the product and provide a compliant label and SDS when they sell or import it.'),
('An employer imports a hazardous product for use only in its own workplace. What is it treated as?',
 'An importer, with supplier duties', ['A worker, with no new duties to meet', 'An end user, fully exempt', 'A retailer, with no duties'],
 "An employer who imports a hazardous product for its own use takes on the supplier's duties, so it must make sure the product has a compliant label and SDS."),
('Which is an employer duty under WHMIS?',
 'Educate and train workers', ['Classify products for sale', 'Approve trade secret claims', 'Write the GHS criteria'],
 "Employers must educate and train workers, keep SDSs available, make sure products are labelled and make workplace labels when needed. Classifying products for sale is the supplier's job."),
('Which is a worker duty under WHMIS?',
 'Take part in WHMIS training', ["Write the product's SDS", 'Classify each new product', 'Approve trade secret claims'],
 'Workers must take part in training, follow safe work procedures, check labels and SDSs and tell the supervisor about missing or damaged labels.'),
('What are the two main hazard groups in WHMIS?',
 'Physical and health hazards', ['Fire and chemical hazards', 'Acute and chronic hazards', 'Toxic and corrosive hazards'],
 'WHMIS sorts hazards into physical hazards (such as flammable or oxidizing) and health hazards (such as acute toxicity or carcinogenicity). The GHS environmental classes were not adopted.'),
('Which GHS hazard group did Canada not adopt as mandatory under WHMIS?',
 'Environmental', ['Health', 'Physical', 'Biohazardous infectious'],
 'Canada did not adopt the GHS environmental hazard classes under WHMIS, so the environment pictogram is not required on WHMIS labels.'),
('Which product type is excluded from WHMIS label and SDS rules because another law covers it?',
 'Pest control products', ['Industrial solvents', 'Compressed oxygen', 'Floor-stripping concentrate'],
 'Pest control products fall under the Pest Control Products Act. Industrial solvents, compressed gases and cleaning concentrates sold to workplaces are covered by WHMIS.'),
('A cleaning product bought at a retail store is a consumer product. What must the employer still do?',
 'Train workers on it', ['Write a new 16-section SDS', 'File a trade secret claim', 'Nothing, it is fully exempt'],
 'Excluded products are exempt from WHMIS labels and SDSs, but employers must still train workers on hazards, safe use and storage under OHS law.'),
('Which of these is excluded from WHMIS supplier requirements?',
 'Manufactured articles', ['Paint thinner in drums', 'Welding gas cylinders', 'Acid sold to a factory'],
 'Manufactured articles, such as a finished tool or pipe, are excluded. Paint thinner, welding gases and industrial acids sold to workplaces are hazardous products under WHMIS.'),
('Explosives are excluded from WHMIS supplier rules. Which law covers them instead?',
 'The Explosives Act', ['The Fire Code only', 'The Pest Control Products Act', 'The Food and Drugs Act'],
 'Explosives are regulated under the federal Explosives Act. The other acts cover fire safety, pesticides and foods, drugs and cosmetics.'),
('Which law covers drugs, cosmetics and food rather than WHMIS?',
 'The Food and Drugs Act', ['The Explosives Act', 'The Hazardous Products Act', 'The Pest Control Products Act'],
 'Cosmetics, devices, drugs and food are excluded from WHMIS because the Food and Drugs Act regulates them.'),
('How is hazardous waste handled under WHMIS?',
 'Store safely and train', ['It needs a full supplier label', 'It needs a 16-section SDS', 'It is left out of all training'],
 'Hazardous waste is excluded from supplier label and SDS rules, but employers must still identify it, store and handle it safely and train workers on it.'),
('What does CBI stand for on an SDS?',
 'Confidential business information', ['Chemical burn index', 'Certified batch identification', 'Combined bulk inventory'],
 "CBI is confidential business information, such as an ingredient's exact identity, which a supplier may withhold only with an approved claim."),
("Who approves a supplier's claim to withhold an ingredient's identity as a trade secret?",
 'Health Canada', ['The provincial OHS regulator', 'The employer buying it', 'Transport Canada'],
 "Only Health Canada approves confidential business information claims, under the Hazardous Materials Information Review Act. The SDS then shows the claim's registry number."),
('When did the amended Hazardous Products Regulations come into force?',
 'December 2022', ['February 2015', 'December 2018', 'January 2026'],
 'The amendments came into force on December 15, 2022, and were published in the Canada Gazette, Part II, on January 4, 2023. February 2015 was when WHMIS 2015 itself began.'),
('When did the transition period for the 2022 HPR amendments end?',
 'December 14, 2025', ['December 1, 2018', 'December 15, 2022', 'November 19, 2027'],
 'Suppliers had three years, to December 14, 2025, to switch to the amended rules. During the transition they could follow the old or new rules, but not a mix of both.'),
('Which GHS revision do the 2022 HPR amendments mainly align WHMIS with?',
 'The 7th revised edition', ['The 3rd revised edition', 'The 5th revised edition', 'The 10th revised edition'],
 'The amendments align WHMIS with the 7th revised edition of the GHS and some provisions of the 8th.'),
('During the 2022 amendment transition, how could a supplier comply?',
 'Old or new rules, not a mix', ['Any mix of old and new rules', 'Only new rules from day one', 'Only the WHMIS 1988 rules'],
 'During the transition, a label and SDS had to follow either the former HPR or the amended HPR in full. Combining parts of each was not allowed.'),
('What replaced WHMIS 1988?',
 'WHMIS 2015', ['WHMIS 2000', 'TDG 2008', 'WHMIS 2010'],
 'WHMIS 2015 replaced the original WHMIS 1988 when Canada adopted the GHS, with full workplace transition by December 2018.'),
('Which statement about WHMIS 1988 and WHMIS 2015 is true?',
 'The 2015 version replaced it', ['Both still run side by side', 'WHMIS 1988 is still required', 'WHMIS 2015 dropped all labels'],
 'The 1988 symbols and MSDS format were replaced by GHS pictograms and the 16-section SDS. Suppliers and employers had to finish the switch by the end of 2018.'),
('Who must the employer consult when planning WHMIS training, where one exists?',
 'The health and safety committee', ["The product's supplier", "Health Canada's inspection staff", 'The local fire marshal'],
 'OHS laws generally require the employer to consult the joint health and safety committee or representative on the WHMIS education and training program.'),
('Who does WHMIS training have to cover?',
 'All exposed workers', ['Only supervisors on site', 'Only newly hired workers', 'Only the office staff'],
 'Every worker who works with or near a hazardous product, or may be exposed to it, must be educated and trained before working with it.'),
('What is general WHMIS education about?',
 'How the system works', ["The site's spill procedure", "A product's storage room", "The plant's alarm codes"],
 'General education covers how WHMIS works: hazard classes, labels, pictograms and SDSs. Workplace-specific training covers the procedures for the products at that site.'),
('What is workplace-specific WHMIS training about?',
 'Procedures for products on site', ['The history of the GHS', 'How Health Canada audits suppliers', 'Writing a supplier SDS'],
 'Workplace-specific training covers safe use, storage, handling, emergency and spill procedures for the hazardous products at that workplace.'),
('How often must an employer review WHMIS training?',
 'At least yearly in most places', ['Only when a worker asks for it', "Once in a worker's career", 'Every five years'],
 'Most jurisdictions require a review at least once a year, and again when conditions or products change. The exact rule varies by jurisdiction.'),
('How can an employer know WHMIS training worked?',
 'Check workers can apply it', ['Count the hours of video', 'Ask the supplier to sign off', 'Post the SDS binder'],
 'WHMIS training must be effective: workers should be able to find and use the information on labels and SDSs and follow the procedures. Hours watched do not prove that.'),
]
